Description
-----------

The plugin is a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server. It offers every SVDRP
command of VDR and of the loaded plugins as an MCP tool, so an AI assistant
like Claude can list timers, search the EPG or read recordings of this VDR.

The tools are built at runtime from SVDRP HELP: VDR's own commands are named
vdr_<CMD> (vdr_LSTT, vdr_NEWT, ...), plugin commands <plugin>_<CMD>
(epgsearch_LSTS, satip_INFO, ...). The help page of each command is the tool
description, the only argument "args" takes the command arguments exactly as
SVDRP expects them. Only the plugins that are loaded show up.

Transport and security
----------------------

The server listens on 127.0.0.1:6420 and speaks MCP over a plain TCP stream,
one JSON-RPC message per line (the MCP stdio format). It has no
authentication of its own. Access from another machine goes through SSH,
which takes care of encryption and login:

  claude mcp add vdr -- ssh <user>@<vdr-host> socat STDIO TCP:127.0.0.1:6420

The VDR host needs socat for that. The commands are sent to the local SVDRP
port, so 127.0.0.1 must be allowed in svdrphosts.conf (the default).

Commands that change or delete things can be hidden with --deny. The default
is DEL*,HITK: DEL* hides DELC, DELR, DELT and every plugin command starting with
DEL, HITK the remote control keys. A single plugin command is denied as
PLUGIN.CMD, e.g. epgsearch.UPDS.
--deny= without a list offers every command. The Debian package ships
/etc/vdr/conf.avail/mcp.conf with --deny=DEL*,HITK, so the file shows what
is denied; without the option the plugin uses the same list.

MCP over HTTPS (clients without SSH)
------------------------------------

With --http=ADDR:PORT, e.g. --http=0.0.0.0:6421, the plugin also speaks MCP's
"Streamable HTTP" transport at https://<vdr-host>:6421/mcp, for MCP clients
that have no SSH: other machines in the LAN, agents, automation tools. It is
off by default. The local server on 127.0.0.1:6420 stays as it is.

Every request needs an API key in the header "Authorization: Bearer <key>".
Keys are managed over SVDRP on the VDR host:

  svdrpsend PLUG mcp KEYADD claude rw    creates a key, shown only once
  svdrpsend PLUG mcp KEYLIST             name, role, date
  svdrpsend PLUG mcp KEYDEL claude       revokes it at once

The plugin stores only the SHA-256 hash of a key, in
/var/lib/vdr/plugins/mcp/keys. MCP clients cannot reach these commands.

A key is either rw or ro. rw may use every command that is not on the deny
list. ro may only list and show: HELP, LSTC, LSTD, LSTE, LSTR, LSTT, NEXT,
PING, STAT, vdr_log and vdr_marks; --ro-allow=LIST adds more, e.g.
--ro-allow=epgsearch.LSTS,epgsearch.QRYS,satip.INFO.

TLS: at the first start the plugin creates a self-signed certificate for the
host names and IPv4 addresses of the VDR (EC P-256, 10 years) in
/var/lib/vdr/plugins/mcp/cert.pem, the log shows its SHA-256 fingerprint. Copy
it to the client and trust it there; for Claude Code (Node.js):

  scp <vdr-host>:/var/lib/vdr/plugins/mcp/cert.pem vdr-mcp.pem
  NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/path/to/vdr-mcp.pem
  claude mcp add --transport http vdr https://<vdr-host>:6421/mcp \
    --header "Authorization: Bearer <key>"

KEYADD prints this command line ready to copy, with the host name, port and
the new key. To replace a key, KEYDEL it and KEYADD it again; KEYADD never
overwrites an existing key.

Delete cert.pem and key.pem and restart VDR for a new certificate, e.g. after
a change of the host name. --cert=FILE and --key=FILE use your own one.
--http-plain switches TLS off, only for tests: the keys then travel
unencrypted.

The server answers each POST with application/json (no server-sent events),
GET is refused with 405, DELETE ends a session. Requests carrying an Origin
header are refused, as browsers send one and MCP clients do not (protection
against DNS rebinding). Do not expose the port to the internet; use a VPN.

System log
----------

With --log the server offers one more tool, vdr_log: the newest lines of the
systemd journal of VDR (unit vdr.service, or the one given as --log=UNIT),
optionally filtered by a text. The tool is off by default, because VDR runs as
the user vdr, which may not read the journal. To use it:

  adduser vdr systemd-journal

and restart VDR. Note that VDR can then read the whole system journal, not
only its own lines. The package does not change any group by itself.

Recordings beyond SVDRP
-----------------------

SVDRP can list, move, cut and replay recordings, but it cannot show or set
editing marks, and it cannot regenerate the index. Three tools fill that gap:

  vdr_marks     editing marks of a recording, its length before and after
                cutting
  vdr_setmarks  replaces all editing marks (h:mm:ss[.ff]), aligned to I-frames
  vdr_genindex  regenerates the index in the background like vdr --genindex
                and thereby counts the errors of the recording (line O of
                vdr_LSTR <id>, -1 = not checked)

vdr_setmarks and vdr_genindex refuse recordings that are being recorded,
replayed or cut; vdr_genindex works on one recording at a time. Each of the
three can be hidden with --deny, e.g. --deny=DEL*,HITK,vdr_setmarks.

A typical chain: vdr_LSTR <id> path, markad_MARK <path>, wait until
markad_STATUS reports no running markad, vdr_marks <id> to check the marks,
then vdr_EDIT <id>. Note that the reply of markad_STATUS when it is done
("no active recording with running markad found") contains the word "running".

Options
-------

  -b ADDR,   --bind=ADDR        address to listen on (default: 127.0.0.1),
                                other addresses are reachable WITHOUT
                                authentication
  -p PORT,   --port=PORT        TCP port of the MCP server (default: 6420)
  -s PORT,   --svdrp-port=PORT  SVDRP port of this VDR (default: 6419)
  -d LIST,   --deny=LIST        SVDRP commands that are not offered
                                (default: DEL*,HITK)
  -m BYTES,  --max-reply=BYTES  cut longer SVDRP replies (default: 262144)
  -l,        --log[=UNIT]       offer the tool vdr_log with the journal of the
                                systemd unit UNIT (default: vdr.service)
  -w ADDR:PORT, --http=ADDR:PORT  MCP over HTTPS with API keys (default: off)
  -c FILE,   --cert=FILE        own TLS certificate
  -k FILE,   --key=FILE         its private key
  -n,        --http-plain       HTTP without TLS, only for tests
  -r LIST,   --ro-allow=LIST    more commands for read only keys
